[2026-03-16T00:45:57.903Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012aBSoYWRRiQsbeoT8YhrV4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! Direct fetch to `https://ep-proud-block-a6ev7gw6.us-west-2.aws.neon.tech/sql` works (returns 400 because we're missing the proper auth headers, but the connection works). The neon driver is still failing with ECONNREFUSED to localhost.\n\nThis means the neon driver is NOT using the fetch endpoint. It's probably falling back to a WebSocket connection, which connects to localhost.\n\nLet me check what the neon driver actually does. In `@neondatabase/serverless` v0.10.4, the `neon()` function should use the HTTP fetch API. But maybe it's falling back to WebSocket for some reason.\n \nAh, I see the issue now—the `fetchEndpoint` function returns a centralized API URL (`https://api.us-west-2.aws.neon.tech/sql`), but the direct fetch I tested uses the per-endpoint URL (`https://ep-proud-block-a6ev7gw6.us-west-2.aws.neon.tech/sql`). These are different endpoints entirely, so the neon driver might be using the wrong URL or falling back to WebSocket instead of HTTP.\n\nThe random port numbers in the ECONNREFUSED errors (34663, 42557) are a dead giveaway that it's attempting a TCP connection, not an HTTP request. The neon driver is probably using the `pg` module internally and trying to establish a TLS connection to the database rather than making an HTTP fetch call like it's supposed to.\n\nMaybe in v0.10.4 there's a fallback mechanism to TCP/WebSocket, or the `fetchOptions: {}` I added isn't actually forcing fetch mode. Let me try passing an explicit `fetchFunction` option to the `neon()` call instead.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1058,"cache_read_input_tokens":114354,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1058,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"s