ondition about `writable` and `readable` being undefined would be true, but then it fails on the `.pipe()` check since Web ReadableStreams don't have that method. Unless Node.js 22+ or `lambda-local` has augmented it somehow. Let me approach this differently and look at what `invocationResult` actually is.\n\nThe issue is that `invocationResult` is a Response object, and spreading it with `{ ...invocationResult, streamPort }` won't work as expected because Response properties are mostly getters on the prototype rather than own enumerable properties. So the spread would likely produce an empty object or just the `streamPort`, meaning `result.body` on the parent side would be undefined while `result?.streamPort` would have the value.\n\nThen when `webResponseFromLambdaResponse` is called with this incomplete `lambdaResponse` object, it tries to access `lambdaResponse.body` which would be undefined, causing issues when constructing the new Response. So the response gets a 200 status code by default, which makes `response.ok` true, but then `response.json()` fails trying to parse an empty body. The error gets caught as a raw rejection rather than a `rejectWithValue`, leaving `state.error` undefined, which is why the component displays \"Property not found\" as the fallback. The real issue is that spreading the Response object in the Worker thread loses the body and status entirely—this is a bug in the `@netlify/functions-dev` library.\n\nI could convert the functions from V2 to V1 format to work around this, but that's a massive refactor across 11 functions. Plus, the ECONNREFUSED error I'm seeing doesn't quite fit this explanation—if the spread operation was losing the streamPort, the parent wouldn't even attempt a connection. Something else is going on here.\n\nLet me reconsider what `lambdaLocal.execute()` actually returns and how it handles Response objects, since that's likely where the streamPort value is coming from in the first place.","signature":""}],"stop_rea