[2026-03-16T00:54:19.565Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T5Bg9Ab6xZHrXnSk58fNLY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the critical line:```javascript\nvar result = lambdaFunc[lambdaHandler](event, ctx, ctx.done);\n```\n\nWhere `lambdaHandler` is `'handler'` (default). But the V2 function has `export default` not `export { handler }`. So `lambdaFunc.handler` would be `undefined` and `lambdaFunc.handler(event, ctx, ctx.done)` would throw \"undefined is not a function\".\n\nBut wait, the worker.js explicitly uses `lambdaFunc`:```javascript\nconst lambdaFunc = await import(entryFilePath)\n\n\n```\n\nAnd then passes it to `lambdaLocal.execute()`, which eventually calls `_executeSync` at line 355 where it tries to access `lambdaFunc[lambdaHandler]`. Since the worker doesn't specify a `lambdaHandler`, it defaults to `'handler'`, but the imported module only has `default` exported, not a named `handler` export.\n\nThe issue is that when ZISI builds a V2 function with the `zisi_pure_esm` flag, the output is in ESM format where `export default` creates a default export accessible as `lambdaFunc.default`, leaving `lambdaFunc.handler` undefined. So the invocation would fail because it's looking for the wrong export.\n\nLet me check if ZISI has special handling to transform `export default` into a `handler` export as well, which would be the standard pattern for Lambda functions.\n\nActually, looking at how ZISI handles V2 functions, it does transform the export to re-export the default as `handler`, so when the worker imports the built file, `lambdaFunc.handler` is properly defined and gets called by lambda-local with the event as the first argument. But then the function tries to access `request.url` when the Lambda event object has `rawUrl` instead.\n\nI'm overcomplicating this. The real issue is that the function executes fine and returns results, but the netlify dev runtime fails when handling the response with an ECONNREFUSED error to a random localhost port. This happens across all functions, not just one. Rather than keep debugging the runtime, I should just try converting the functions to V1 format where they export a handler that returns an object with statusCode and body.\n\nBut with 11 function files to convert, let me try a quicker approach first: I'll patch the worker.js in node_modules to prevent it from treating the body as a stream.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":803,"cache_read_input_tokens":153610,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":803,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd78e25b-7fce-4bae-a91a-1f392b40d970","uuid":"4ce366c9-1cf7-41c0-ac26-a5b23eca041f"}
