[2026-03-16T01:09:34.962Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ABuhtNi9pH8RfVdHHNzy4Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the flow completely!\n\n1. `lambda-local` provides `global.awslambda` with `streamifyResponse` and `HttpResponseStream`\n2. `streamifyResponse` wraps the handler, creating a `StreamingBody` (which extends `PassThrough` - a Node.js stream)\n3. The bootstrap's `getLambdaHandler` calls `awslambda.streamifyResponse(handler)` which returns a wrapped handler\n4. When the wrapped handler is called:\n   - It creates a `StreamingBody` (a PassThrough stream)\n   - Calls the original handler with `(event, body, context)` where `body` is the StreamingBody\n   - The handler writes the response to the StreamingBody, `HttpResponseStream.from(body, metadata)` sends the headers, and the body data flows through the stream\n5. The result returned to `lambdaLocal.execute()` is `{statusCode, headers, body: StreamingBody}` where the body is a PassThrough stream with a `pipe` method\n6. In worker.js, `isStream(invocationResult.body)` detects this as a stream and handles it accordingly\n7. The worker creates a TCP server on a random port to stream the body, posts the result with the streamPort to the parent, then may exit\n8. The parent connects to `localhost:streamPort` to read the body\n\nNow I'm seeing the race condition—if the worker thread exits before the parent connects, or if the TCP server closes prematurely, the parent gets ECONNREFUSED when trying to establish that connection. I see the real problem now—the PassThrough stream might already be fully written and ended before the TCP server even gets created, so when the parent finally connects and tries to pipe from it, the stream has nothing left to send or it's already closed.\n\nActually, PassThrough does buffer data, so that should be fine. The actual issue is that the worker thread exits immediately after posting the message to the parent. Even though the TCP server is listening and should keep the event loop alive, once there's no more work queued up, the worker might be terminating before the parent has a chance to connect and establish the socket connection.\n\nLet me reconsider the flow here. When the parent connects and pipes the response body through the socket, if the body has already finished streaming, the pipe would flush all buffered data and then trigger the 'end' event, which closes the server. That part should work fine.\n\nBut I'm realizing the real problem might be timing around when `global.awslambda` gets initialized. The worker imports the lambda-local module, which should set up that global object, but I need to verify when exactly that happens relative to when the entry point tries to call `awslambda.streamifyResponse()`.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2705,"cache_read_input_tokens":101015,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2705,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd78e25b-7fce-4bae-a91a-1f392b40d970","uuid":"6a045d6e-377f-4cd5-96dd-0294db2138e7"}
