[2026-03-16T01:10:49.937Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_012Ny9Y1Nyauoc3z22ucixhX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I see the full picture clearly:\n\n1. For V2 functions (runtimeAPIVersion === 2): Worker thread executes the function, returns result with streaming body. Parent connects via TCP to read the stream.\n\n2. For V1 functions (runtimeAPIVersion !== 2): `invokeFunctionDirectly` is called - runs in the MAIN process, no worker threads.\n\n3. `webResponseFromLambdaResponse` creates a `new Response(lambdaResponse.body, ...)` - this takes the body from the lambda result.\n\nFor V2 (streaming path):\n- Worker posts `{statusCode, headers, streamPort}` (body removed from message)\n\n\n- Parent connects to the TCP port and gets a Node.js stream as the response body\n- For V1 (direct path), the result already contains the body as a string, so it's wrapped directly in the Response\n\nThe ECONNREFUSED error is likely happening because the worker's TCP server closes before the parent can establish the connection—possibly the body stream ends and triggers server shutdown prematurely.\n\nI'm noticing my debug logging in main.js isn't showing up in the test output, even though other WebServer logs are captured. The function runtime output seems to be missing entirely, which suggests either the logging isn't reaching the test output or the function isn't executing as expected.\n\nIf the function isn't being invoked, the redirect rule would fail to find it, and the request might fall through to the Vite dev server, which would then serve the SPA via the catch-all redirect. That would explain why the browser shows \"Property not found\" instead of an actual error.\n\nLet me think about why the functions wouldn't be found in the first place. I notice there's debug code in main.js that should be appearing in the test output but isn't, which suggests either the functions aren't being invoked or the console output is going somewhere else. I should clean up that debug code and try clearing the `.netlify/functions-serve/` directory to rule out stale builds.\n\nLooking at the pattern of which tests pass versus fail, I'm noticing something: the passing tests hit generic endpoints like `/api/bookings` and `/api/auth`, while the failing ones all hit `/api/properties/<id>` with a dynamic ID. The properties function itself returned data when I checked the debug logs earlier, so the issue might be more specific to how that endpoint handles the response or how the SPA processes it.\n\nI'm going to step back and try a cleaner diagnostic approach: reinstall node_modules to remove my debug code, then modify the properties function to return a hardcoded static response before any database calls. If that static response makes it through to the test, I'll know the problem isn't in the function execution itself—it's somewhere in the database layer or response handling.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2191,"cache_read_input_tokens":107285,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2191,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd78e25b-7fce-4bae-a91a-1f392b40d970","uuid":"bca547d7-3067-4e23-a087-10816ab77bce"}
