e pipeline continues asynchronously in the background. The worker then creates a TCP server and posts the port, and when the parent connects, the server starts piping the body data through the socket—but there's a timing issue here where the body might not be fully available yet.\n\nDefining the wrapper function... I see the transpiled generator code that handles the async flow — it's wrapping the handler invocation in a try-catch block using the generator pattern, where it yields to the handler, captures the metadata response, sends headers if they haven't been sent yet, and then calls the invocation end hook, or rejects with any errors that occur.\n\nThe key insight is that `HttpResponseStream.from` triggers `sendHeader` which resolves the outer promise while the handler is still executing, so `lambdaLocal.execute()` returns before the streaming pipeline actually completes.\n\nOnce the worker receives this result, it checks if the body is a stream and sets up a TCP server. The PassThrough stream acts as a buffer—the bootstrap continues piping response data into it asynchronously while the worker's TCP server pipes that data out to clients as they connect.\n\nNow I'm wondering what happens if the worker tries to read from the stream before the bootstrap has finished writing to it...\n\nor worse, if the PassThrough ends before any client connects. If the bootstrap completes its pipeline and the stream closes before the TCP client attaches, the data would be lost since there's no downstream consumer yet. I'm realizing the confusion here—`socket.on('end', ...)` listens for when the remote end closes the connection by sending FIN, but since this is a server socket being written to, the parent won't send FIN until it closes its side, which might not happen until after the data finishes piping.\n\nBut that still doesn't explain the ECONNREFUSED error. Let me reconsider whether the issue is actually with how the `is-stream` library detects streams—maybe the version being 