[2026-03-16T01:19:58.825Z] {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01QqwsodgSj6yniqaevVNPiZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so `isStream` checks: `stream.writable || stream.readable || !checkOpen || ...` and `typeof stream.pipe === 'function'`. A `StreamingBody` extends `PassThrough`, which has both `.writable` and `.readable` properties, and a `.pipe` method. So `isStream` returns `true`.\n\nBut wait - the PassThrough might have `.readable === false` if the stream has already ended. Let me check:\n\nIf the bootstrap has finished piping all data to the PassThrough, the PassThrough's readable state might be `false` (ended). In `is-stream v4.0.1`, `isStream` with `checkOpen: true` (default) would check `stream.writable || stream.readable || ...`. If both are false (stream ended on both ends), it checks the next condition: `!checkOpen` (which is `false`) or `(stream.writable === undefined && stream.readable === undefined)` (which is `false`).\n\n\nSo if the stream has ended, `isStream` returns `false`, and the PassThrough gets sent directly via `parentPort.postMessage`. Since PassThrough objects can't be serialized, this would either result in an empty object or throw a DataCloneError.\n\nBut actually, the timing matters here - `lambdaLocal.execute()` returns when `sendHeader` is called, which happens before the pipeline finishes. At that point the PassThrough should still be readable since data is still flowing through it.\n\nUnless the response is tiny and the pipeline completes synchronously right after `sendHeader`, in which case the PassThrough might already be closed by the time `isStream` checks it. Even then, sending an unserializable body via `postMessage` would fail, leaving the parent without a `streamPort` and causing `webResponseFromLambdaResponse` to create a Response with an object it can't handle.\n\nRather than chase down the exact race condition, I think the simplest approach is to convert these V2 functions to V1 format instead.\n\nActually, that's too invasive. Let me look at the `invokeFunction` logic more carefully—it checks `runtimeAPIVersion` to decide between the V1 path (direct invocation) and the V2 path (worker thread with streaming). If I can figure out what determines that version, maybe I can configure it differently.\n\nThe thing is, even if I force V1, the lambda handler itself still calls `awslambda.streamifyResponse`, so the streaming would still happen. But in V1, the invocation runs in the main process, so the `StreamingBody` gets returned directly to `webResponseFromLambdaResponse`, which wraps it in a Web API Response. Newer Node.js versions can handle Node.js streams in the Response constructor, so that might actually work—but I need to verify whether `invokeFunctionDirectly` actually supports this.\n\nWait, `invokeFunctionDirectly` only works for V1 functions anyway, not V2. Let me try a different approach instead: I'll modify worker.js to fully consume the stream into a string before sending it via the message, rather than trying to stream it over TCP.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":522,"cache_read_input_tokens":125563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":522,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd78e25b-7fce-4bae-a91a-1f392b40d970","uuid":"5b49a2e6-4239-4b16-80f5-90752c55a21f"}
